### What is the Evaluate Expression problem?

Given a string expression containing numbers and the operators +, -, and *, compute all possible results from all valid ways of grouping (parenthesizing) the expression. Return them as a list.

At National Bank of India, a fee formula like "2*3-4*5" can be grouped as ((2*3)-(4*5)) = -14 or (2*(3-(4*5))) = -34 and other ways. Finance wants every possible result to see the range.

```java
package com.bank.formula;

import java.util.*;

public class DiffWaysToCompute {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> results = diffWaysToCompute("2*3-4*5");
        Collections.sort(results);
        System.out.println("All results: " + results);
    }

    static Map<String, List<Integer>> memo = new HashMap<>();

    static List<Integer> diffWaysToCompute(String expr) {
        if (memo.containsKey(expr)) return memo.get(expr);
        List<Integer> results = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < expr.length(); i++) {
            char c = expr.charAt(i);
            if (c == '+' || c == '-' || c == '*') {
                List<Integer> left = diffWaysToCompute(expr.substring(0, i));
                List<Integer> right = diffWaysToCompute(expr.substring(i + 1));
                for (int l : left) {
                    for (int r : right) {
                        results.add(apply(l, r, c));
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        if (results.isEmpty()) {
            results.add(Integer.parseInt(expr));  // base case: plain number
        }

        memo.put(expr, results);
        return results;
    }

    static int apply(int a, int b, char op) {
        return switch (op) {
            case '+' -> a + b;
            case '-' -> a - b;
            case '*' -> a * b;
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown op: " + op);
        };
    }
}
```

```text
All results: [-34, -14, -10, -10, 10]
```

### How does the divide-and-conquer recursion work?

Scan the expression character by character. At each operator, treat it as the top-level operation of one grouping. Recursively compute all results of the left substring and all results of the right substring. Then combine every left result with every right result using the operator.

```text
Expression: "2*3-4"

Split at '*' (index 1):
  left = "2" -> [2]
  right = "3-4" -> [-1, ...] (recurse)
  Combine: 2 * (-1) = -2

Split at '-' (index 3):
  left = "2*3" -> [6] (recurse)
  right = "4" -> [4]
  Combine: 6 - 4 = 2

Results: [-2, 2]
```

### Why is memoization important?

The number of distinct groupings follows the Catalan number sequence. For n operators, there are C(n) groupings where C(1)=1, C(2)=2, C(3)=5, C(4)=14. Without memoization, overlapping sub-expressions are recomputed many times. Caching by substring key ensures each sub-expression is solved once.

```java
// Without memo: exponential recomputation
// "2*3-4*5" splits at each operator, and sub-expressions overlap

// With memo: each unique substring computed once
static Map<String, List<Integer>> memo = new HashMap<>();
if (memo.containsKey(expr)) return memo.get(expr);
// ... compute ...
memo.put(expr, results);
```

### How do you use BigDecimal for exact banking arithmetic?

In a banking context, use `BigDecimal` instead of `int` to keep all arithmetic exact and avoid floating-point errors. Replace `+`, `-`, `*` with `add`, `subtract`, `multiply`.

```java
package com.bank.formula;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class DiffWaysBigDecimal {

    static Map<String, List<BigDecimal>> memo = new HashMap<>();

    static List<BigDecimal> diffWays(String expr) {
        if (memo.containsKey(expr)) return memo.get(expr);
        List<BigDecimal> results = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < expr.length(); i++) {
            char c = expr.charAt(i);
            if (c == '+' || c == '-' || c == '*') {
                for (BigDecimal l : diffWays(expr.substring(0, i))) {
                    for (BigDecimal r : diffWays(expr.substring(i + 1))) {
                        results.add(applyOp(l, r, c));
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if (results.isEmpty()) results.add(new BigDecimal(expr));
        memo.put(expr, results);
        return results;
    }

    static BigDecimal applyOp(BigDecimal a, BigDecimal b, char op) {
        return switch (op) {
            case '+' -> a.add(b);
            case '-' -> a.subtract(b);
            case '*' -> a.multiply(b);
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<BigDecimal> results = diffWays("100*3-50*2");
        Collections.sort(results);
        System.out.println("Fee formula results: " + results);
    }
}
```

```text
Fee formula results: [-9700, -100, 100, 200, 500]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The number of results for n operators is the Catalan number C(n), which grows as O(4^n / n^(3/2)). Memoization ensures each of the O(n^2) unique substrings is computed once, but the total number of results across all substrings is still exponential.

| Metric | Complexity |
|--------|------------|
| Unique substrings | O(n^2) |
| Results per expression | Catalan(operators) |
| Time with memo | O(n^2 * C(n)) roughly |
| Space | O(n^2 * C(n)) for memo |

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single number (no operators), an expression with only one operator, and multi-digit numbers.

```java
package com.bank.formula;

import java.util.List;

public class EdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Single number
        System.out.println(DiffWaysToCompute.diffWaysToCompute("42"));  // [42]

        // One operator
        System.out.println(DiffWaysToCompute.diffWaysToCompute("3+5")); // [8]

        // Multi-digit numbers
        System.out.println(DiffWaysToCompute.diffWaysToCompute("10+20")); // [30]
    }
}
```

```text
[42]
[8]
[30]
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is trying to use `Integer.parseInt` on the whole string before checking for operators, which fails for multi-operator expressions. The correct approach is to try splitting at every operator position first, and only parse as a number when no operator is found.

```java
// WRONG: parse first, split later
int num = Integer.parseInt(expr);  // throws for "2+3"

// CORRECT: split at operators; parse only if no operator found
for (int i = 0; i < expr.length(); i++) {
    if (isOperator(expr.charAt(i))) {
        // split and recurse
    }
}
if (results.isEmpty()) {
    results.add(Integer.parseInt(expr));  // safe: no operators
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Catalan numbers.** The growth rate matters: for 10 operators there are 16,796 groupings. For practical expressions this is fine, but for very long expressions the output itself is exponential.
- **Division operator.** Adding division requires handling division by zero. In a banking formula evaluator, division would use BigDecimal with a specified scale and rounding mode.
- **Expression trees.** Each grouping corresponds to a distinct binary expression tree. This problem enumerates all structurally distinct trees.
- **Production formula engines.** In production, formulas are parsed once into an AST, not re-evaluated by string splitting. The divide-and-conquer approach demonstrates the concept but is not the production implementation.
